To remove the strict 2-decimal rule for INR and allow up to 4 decimal places for *all* currencies universally, you just need to delete that else if condition.
Open your **VoucherRuleValidator.java** file and locate the runFormatCheck method.
**Find this exact block of code:**
```java
            if (d.amount == null || d.amount.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) <= 0) {
                synchronized(d.formatErrors) { d.formatErrors.add("Amount must be greater than 0."); }
            } else {
                if (d.amount.precision() > 20 || d.amount.scale() > 4) {
                    synchronized(d.formatErrors) { d.formatErrors.add("Exceeded maximum digits or decimal places."); }
                } else if ("INR".equalsIgnoreCase(d.currency) && d.amount.stripTrailingZeros().scale() > 2) {
                    synchronized(d.formatErrors) { d.formatErrors.add("Exceeded maximum digits or decimal places."); }
                }
            }

```
**Replace it with this:**
```java
            if (d.amount == null || d.amount.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) <= 0) {
                synchronized(d.formatErrors) { d.formatErrors.add("Amount must be greater than 0."); }
            } else if (d.amount.precision() > 20 || d.amount.scale() > 4) {
                synchronized(d.formatErrors) { d.formatErrors.add("Exceeded maximum 16 digits or 4 decimal places."); }
            }

```
This cleaner version will now blindly accept up to 4 decimal places (e.g., 55000.1234) for every single currency code without throwing an error.
